# Tokens for `cookie_verify`. The input is
#   u16 tamper | u16 cut | name \0 secret \0 other_secret \0 hostile \0 value
# and four of the five fields are NUL-terminated, so the separator is the
# single most valuable token here: without it every input is one enormous
# cookie name and `value` is never reached.
"\x00"
"\x00\x00"

# --- the signed-string grammar: base64url payload "." base64url MAC ----
# The separator `verify` splits on, and the two alphabet characters that
# tell base64url from standard base64.
"."
".."
"-"
"_"
# Standard-base64 characters, which must NOT appear in a signature and so
# make a decode failure easy for the fuzzer to reach.
"+"
"/"
"="
# Signature-shaped runs. A real MAC is 43 characters; these let the fuzzer
# build strings of the right length without discovering the length itself.
"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"
"AAAA"
"AAAAAAAA"

# --- a genuine signed cookie ------------------------------------------
# sign("session", "user-42", "s3cret"), split into its parts so a mutation
# can graft either half onto something else.
"dXNlci00Mg.OFYOV27UObENfcm060qnIV2pgFryh6r1zglatvieHoc"
"dXNlci00Mg"
"OFYOV27UObENfcm060qnIV2pgFryh6r1zglatvieHoc"
"session"
"s3cret"

# --- the strings the MAC is computed over ------------------------------
# The MAC covers `name "=" payload`, so an `=` inside a name is where a
# concatenation ambiguity would live if the payload were ever variable.
"session="
"a="
"=."
